invariant: INV-11
file: quic_initial.c
catches: quic_test
reason: quic_initial_open derives the caller's own endpoint's Initial
  secret instead of the peer's, so a client reads under "client in" and a
  server under "server in" and each endpoint opens only what it wrote
  itself. RFC 9001 §5.2 gives each endpoint its own secret
  (rfc9001.txt:1057-1061), and nothing else in the tree says which of the
  two a direction takes: aes_public_key_initial derives whichever endpoint
  it is handed and returns CH_OK for both, so this edit compiles, derives
  a real key and fails only against a real peer. The seal side is
  untouched, so every A.2 and A.3 packet is still written correctly and a
  test that checked sealing alone stays green.
  RFC 9001 Appendix A is what catches it: test_initial_endpoint_reads
  opens Appendix A.3's server packet as a client and Appendix A.2's client
  packet as a server, and each also refuses the packet it wrote itself.

--- old
    if (endpoint == CH_QUIC_ENDPOINT_CLIENT) {
        return CH_QUIC_ENDPOINT_SERVER;
    }
    if (endpoint == CH_QUIC_ENDPOINT_SERVER) {
        return CH_QUIC_ENDPOINT_CLIENT;
    }
    return endpoint;
--- new
    return endpoint;
